.TH MATH-LINALG 2
.SH NAME
Math: dot, norm1, norm2, iamax, axpby, spmv, lap5, gemm, sort \- linear algebra primitives
.SH SYNOPSIS
.EX
include	"math.m";
math := load Math Math->PATH;

dot: fn(x, y: array of real): real;
norm1, norm2: fn(x: array of real): real;
iamax: fn(x: array of real): int;
axpby: fn(a: real, x: array of real, b: real, y: array of real);
spmv: fn(rowptr, colidx: array of int, val, x, y: array of real);
lap5: fn(nx, ny: int, dx, dy: real, bc: int, x, y: array of real);
gemm: fn(transa, transb: int,  # upper case N or T
		m, n, k: int, alpha: real,
		a: array of real, lda: int,
		b: array of real, ldb: int, beta: real,
		c: array of real, ldc: int);

sort: fn(x: array of real, p: array of int);
.EE
.SH DESCRIPTION
These routines implement the basic functions of linear algebra.
The standard vector inner product and norms are defined as follows:
.IP
.BI dot( "x , y" )
=
.BI sum( x [ i ]* y\fP[\fPi\fP])
.IP
.BI norm1( x )
=
.BI sum(fabs( x [ i\  \f5]))
.IP
.BI norm2( x )
=
.BI sqrt(dot( "x , x" ))
.PP
.PP
.BI axpby( "a , x , b , y" )
replaces
.I y
by
.IB a * x " + " b * y\fP,
in place;
.I x
is only read.
This is the update a Krylov solve does most \(em conjugate gradients
does three per iteration \(em and it is
.I axpby
rather than the more usual
.I axpy
because both shapes are needed: an accumulating
.IB y " += " a * x
for the solution and residual, and
.IB y " = " x " + " b * y
for the search direction, which an accumulating form cannot express
without destroying the residual it needs.
.PP
It is worth using.
The same loop written in Limbo costs about sixteen times as much even
with the JIT enabled, because a streaming multiply\-add vectorises where
the interpreter's generated code does not; substituting it into
.IR krylov (2)
took a 24\(mu24\(mu24
.IR fem (2)
Poisson solve from 54ms to 47ms on the CPU and from 44ms to about 25ms
on the GPU backend, with the iteration count and residual unchanged.
.PP
.PP
.BI spmv( "rowptr , colidx , val , x , y" )
computes
.I y
=
.IR Ax ,
where
.I A
is sparse in compressed sparse row form:
.I rowptr
has one entry per row plus a final total,
.I colidx
and
.I val
one each per nonzero, and
.I y
is as long as there are rows.
Column indices are bounds-checked, and a matrix that does not describe
itself consistently raises the same error as a length mismatch.
.PP
This is the operation a Krylov solve spends nearly all of its time in \(em
at 24\(mu24\(mu24 about 360us of matvec against 12us of everything else \(em
and it is nine to ten times faster than the same loop in Limbo.
The row loop and its summation order are unchanged, so results differ from
the Limbo version only by the compiler contracting a multiply and an add into
a fused one: about two units in the last place, which does not change a CG
iteration count.
.PP
.PP
.BI lap5( "nx , ny , dx , dy , bc , x , y" )
sets
.I y
to the five-point Laplacian of
.I x
on an
.I nx
by
.I ny
grid of spacing
.I dx
by
.IR dy .
Both arrays are
.IR nx * ny
long, laid out by rows, and must not be the same array.
.I Bc
says what lies outside the grid and takes
.IR pde (2)'s
own values: 0 clamps to the edge, 1 wraps, 2 reads zero.
.PP
This is what an implicit PDE solve spends its time in \(em applied once
per Krylov iteration, it was 78 to 82 per cent of a
.IR pde (2)
solve when it was written in Limbo, and replacing it took a
256\(mu256 hundred-step implicit diffusion from 28.1s to 5.3s.
.PP
The interior loop has no boundary test in it; the edges are computed
separately, once.
That is where such a kernel goes wrong, so
.IR pdetest (1)
compares every cell against the obvious version for all three boundary
conditions and for grids one cell wide.
.PP
For the infinity norm, the function
iamax(x)
computes an integer
.I i
such that
.BI fabs( x [ i ])
is maximal.
These are all standard BLAS (basic linear algebra subroutines)
except that in Inferno they apply only to contiguous (unit stride)
vectors.
.PP
We assume the convention that matrices are represented as
singly-subscripted arrays with Fortran storage order.
So for an
.I m
by
.I n
matrix
.I A
we use loops
with
.BI 0\(<= i < m
and
.BI 0\(<= j < n
and access
\f2A\f5[\f2i\f5+\f2m\f5*\f2j\f5]\f1.
.PP
Rather than provide the huge number of entry points in BLAS2 and BLAS3,
Inferno provides the matrix multiply primitive,
.BR gemm ,
defined by
.PP
.EX
    \f2A\fP = \f1\(*a\fP*\f2A\fP\f1'\fP*\f2B\fP\f1'\fP + \f1\(*b\fP*\f2C\fP
.EE
.PP
where the apostrophes indicate an optional transposition.
As shown by the
work of Kagstrom, Ling, and Van Loan, the other BLAS functionality can
be built efficiently on top of
.BR gemm .
.PP
Matrix
.IR a '
is
.I m
by
.IR k ,
matrix
.IR b '
is
.I k
by
.IR n ,
and matrix
.I c
is
.I m
by
.IR n .
Here
.IR a '
means
.I a
.RI ( a ')
if
.I transa
is the constant
.B 'N'
.RB ( 'T' ),
and similarly for
.IR b '.
The sizes
.IR m ,
.IR n ,
and
.I k
denote the `active' part of
the matrix.
The parameters
.IR lda ,
.IR ldb ,
and
.I ldc
denote the `leading dimension'
or size for purposes of indexing.
So to loop over
.I c
use loops
with
.BI 0\(<= i < m
and
.BI 0\(<= j < n
and access
\f2a\f5[\f2i\f5+\f2ldc\f5*\f2j\f5]\f1.
.PP
The sorting function
.BI sort( x , p )
updates a 0-origin permutation
.I p
so that
.IB x [ p [ i ]]
\(<=
.IB x [ p [ i +1]]\f1,
leaving
.I x
unchanged.
